    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build is green: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement was that all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, with unchanged HTTP status codes.\n\n**Findings:** Rejections in this OfficeFloor app are produced by escalation handlers in `src/main/java/.../rest/escalation/`, not `@ControllerAdvice`. The framework-level `ValidationExceptionHandler` (400) already emitted a `ProblemDetail`, but the eight custom owner-rule handlers returned a plain `Map.of(\"errors\", \u2026)` JSON body with no `type`/`title`/`status`/`detail`.\n\n**Change:** I converted all eight to build an RFC7807 body via the existing `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)` helper (which sets `type` = `about:blank`, `title` = exception simple name, `detail`, and `status` via `ProblemDetail.forStatus`), returning `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` exactly like the existing validation handler so Spring emits `application/problem+json`. I preserved the original field-level error info as an extra `errors` property so no information is lost:\n\n- **400:** `MissingRequiredFields`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`, `FutureRegistrationDate`\n- **409:** `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n- **429:** `DailyOwnerLimit`\n\nStatus codes are unchanged. The acceptance test `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (asserting a 400 with `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400`) now passes, and the full suite is green.\n\nNote (not part of this task, but observed while surveying the handlers): `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler` responds `404 NOT_FOUND` despite its name suggesting a conflict \u2014 a likely latent mis-mapping worth a look separately.",
